.TH PCBCHECK 1
.SH NAME
pcbcheck \- check a board against its netlist and rules
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pcbcheck
[
.B \-l
] [
.B \-r
] [
.B \-L
.I lib
] ...
.I board.pcb
.RI < board.lock
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pcbcheck
checks the whole board from its copper alone: pads, tracks and vias
join where they touch on a layer, vias and holes on every layer, and a
via or hole of a plane's net, or a pad or track of it on the plane's
layer, joins the plane (but a pad the plane relieves joins by its neck
alone).
Each net must be one piece and no piece may hold two nets; copper of two
nets keeps the clearance; copper keeps the edge rule from the outline
and the cutouts, measured from the copper itself, a track as the
segment it is, to a cutout's round corners as round, but for the parts
the rule excepts, the fault at the point of the copper nearest the edge
(copper past the outline, or into a cutout, is a fault of its own: it
runs past the edge);
each track ends at the center of a pad of its net (of a long pad,
anywhere on the line between its ends, as
.IR pcbroute (1)
joins it), at a via (or, a neck, in its own plane), or on the copper
between the two last pins of a
.B fork
of its net; the copper
between the two pins of a
.B width
.I PIN PIN W
statement (the fewest pieces from one to the other), and each
connection of the lock, of every net, is no narrower than its width
.RI ( pcb (5):
the width statements, else its class's, else rule track), a neck to a via or plane no narrower than the net's width, or
on a small part of two pins than rule track (one fault a connection);
every via keeps
the via gap from
the pads of its net (a via inside or tangent to its pad is a fault); the
solder mask keeps its web between two openings of two nets, or of one
net apart (a sliver), and between an opening and a via; a small part of
two pins with one pad on a plane joins it by a neck no wider than a
track, with no via by it, or its pads heat unevenly (a thermal
imbalance); no silkscreen, a footprint's lines or a designator, runs
over an opening of the solder mask, where it would be cut, and no two
designators of a side overlap; no two
courtyards on one side overlap, and no courtyard on either side, nor
any via, comes into the keep-out of a hole a screw goes through
.RI ( pcbfp (5)),
whatever the nets, the fault naming both parts
(the courtyards of ch1.tank.l (L401) and ch1.tank.c1 (C402) overlap;
the courtyard of ch1.tank.l (L401) in the keep-out of hole blk.1/7
(Z401)); every part
is placed by a current statement, with the nets of its pads as they are;
and each net's copper was made by its current statements for its pads
where they stand: the fault says what changed.
A designator stands as
.IR pcb (5)
says: by its ref statement, or by the rule, along its courtyard's long
side, as large and as near as fits, the most cramped first.
A fault of a designator over a pad or another designator ends with its
size, the sides it tried, and the least change that clears it at 0.5
mm: a ref statement (; try: ref PART SIDE size 0.5), else a gap of a
place statement within 2 mm (; try: place PART WAY of PART GAP), else
the part in the way (; nothing within 2 mm: move PART or a neighbor
(NAME)).
The text of a text statement must not run over an opening of the mask,
off the board or over a cutout.
Each fault is a line, naming a part by its name and designator
(ch1.gain.rf (R401)) and a pad as ch1.gain.rf.1 (R401), and each once,
however many ways it is found; nothing is printed when all is well.
With
.B \-l
it prints, a net a line, the length of its tracks and its vias, and,
for each part of two pins on two nets, the area of the loop of current
through it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
length NET MM vias N
loop PART REF NET NET MM2
.fi
.RE
.PP
The loop runs from each of the part's pads along the shortest copper
of its net to the nearest pad of another part or via of the net, and
closes straight between those two ends: a decoupling capacitor's loop
from its supply pin to its ground via, a feedback resistor's between
the amplifier's output and input.
With -r it prints, a part a line, the free room on each side of its
courtyard, in mm out to 5, to the courtyards and the openings of the
mask of the other parts of its side, the keep-outs of the holes and the
board's edge rule, for choosing where a ref statement puts its
designator:
.PP
.RS
room PART REF above MM below MM left MM right MM
.RE
.PP
For each unplaced statement it prints a note, and one for each net that
waits for its parts: a net of a plane is checked among its placed pads
alone, any other is not routed yet and may be in pieces; notes are not
faults.
.PP
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
pcbcheck -l outputs.pcb < outputs.lock | sort -k3 -n
pcbcheck -l outputs.pcb < outputs.lock | awk '$1 == "loop"' | sort -k6 -n
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
pcb(5), pcbroute(1), pcb-howto(7)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status 1 on a fault.
